"}],[{"text":"60,000"}],[]],[[{"text":"Depreciation: motor van"}],[{"text":"128,000"}],[]],[[{"text":"Total ex$study_guide$);
insert into private.study_guide_chunks (guide, ordinal, chunk) values ('study_guide_acc101', 33, $study_guide$penses"}],[],[{"text":"(1,036,000)"}]],[[{"text":"Net profit","bold":true}],[],[{"text":"326,000","bold":true}]]]},{"type":"paragraph","text":[{"text":"Balance sheet of Adaeze Stores as at 31 December 2025","bold":true}]},{"type":"table","header":[[{"text":"Non-current assets"}],[{"text":"Cost ₦"}],[{"text":"Acc. depreciation ₦"}],[{"text":"NBV ₦"}]],"rows":[[[{"text":"Equipment"}],[{"text":"600,000"}],[{"text":"180,000"}],[{"text":"420,000"}]],[[{"text":"Motor van"}],[{"text":"800,000"}],[{"text":"288,000"}],[{"text":"512,000"}]],[[],[{"text":"1,400,000"}],[{"text":"468,000"}],[{"text":"932,000","bold":true}]]]},{"type":"table","header":[[],[{"text":"₦"}],[{"text":"₦"}]],"rows":[[[{"text":"Current assets","bold":true}],[],[]],[[{"text":"Stock"}],[{"text":"380,000"}],[]],[[{"text":"Debtors 400,000 less provision 20,000"}],[{"text":"380,000"}],[]],[[{"text":"Prepaid insurance"}],[{"text":"12,000"}],[]],[[{"text":"Cash at bank"}],[{"text":"186,000"}],[]],[[{"text":"Cash in hand"}],[{"text":"14,000"}],[]],[[],[{"text":"972,000"}],[]],[[{"text":"Less: current liabilities","bold":true}],[],[]],[[{"text":"Creditors"}],[{"text":"290,000"}],[]],[[{"text":"Wages owing (accrued)"}],[{"text":"30,000"}],[]],[[],[{"text":"(320,000)"}],[]],[[{"text":"Working capital"}],[],[{"text":"652,000"}]],[[{"text":"Non-current assets + working capital"}],[],[{"text":"1,584,000"}]],[[{"text":"Less: bank loan (non-current liability)"}],[],[{"text":"(300,000)"}]],[[{"text":"Net assets","bold":true}],[],[{"text":"1,284,000","bold":true}]],[[{"text":"Financed by","bold":true}],[],[]],[[{"text":"Capital at 1 January 2025"}],[],[{"text":"1,258,000"}]],[[{"text":"Add: net profit"}],[],[{"text":"326,000"}]],[[],[],[{"text":"1,584,000"}]],[[{"text":"Less: drawings"}],[],[{"text":"(300,000)"}]],[[{"text":"Closing capital","bold":true}],[],[{"text":"1,284,000","bold":true}]]]},{"type":"paragraph","text":[{"text":"Net assets ₦1,284,000 = closing capital ₦1,284,000, so the balance sheet balances. In the equation form: total assets ₦1,904,000 (932,000 + 972,000) = capital ₦1,284,000 + loan ₦300,000 + current liabilities ₦320,000."}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Traps:","bold":true},{"text":" each adjustment appears "},{"text":"twice","bold":true},{"text":" (once in profit and loss, once in the balance sheet). Depreciation on reducing balance is worked on NBV (cost − opening accumulated depreciation), not cost. Only the ₦5,000 increase in provision is charged, but the full ₦20,000 is deducted from debtors. Drawings go against capital, never into expenses."}]}]}]}],"recap":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"text":"Three final accounts: trading account (gross profit), profit and loss account (net profit), balance sheet (position at a date, built on Assets = Liabilities + Capital)."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Cost of sales = opening stock + purchases + carriage inwards − returns outwards − closing stock."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Gross profit = net sales (sales − returns inwards) − cost of sales."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Carriage inwards adds to cost of sales; carriage outwards is a P&L expense."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Net profit = gross profit + other income − expenses; drawings are deducted from capital, not expenses."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Overstated closing stock overstates gross profit."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Current assets turn into cash within a year; intangible assets have no physical form."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Working capital = current assets − current liabilities."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Every adjustment has two effects: one in profit and loss, one in the balance sheet."}]},{"text":[{"text":"Closing capital = opening capital + net profit − drawings, and it equals net assets."}]}]}],"theory":{"itemId":"0cc6c0e3-721d-51f2-9873-a355d6fd11c4","question":[{"type":"paragraph","text":[{"text":"Distinguish between gross profit and net profit. Then compute both from: sales ₦500,000, returns inwards ₦20,000, opening stock ₦60,000, purchases ₦300,000, carriage inwards ₦10,000, carriage outwards ₦8,000, closing stock ₦70,000, rent ₦40,000, drawings ₦25,000."}]}]},"practice":[{"topic":"Final accounts of a sole trader","named":false}]}}],"theory":[{"kind":"theory_question","itemId":"3b826be6-ed6b-554a-ad2e-32f911efb6e0","sourceKey":"guide-theory:1","position":1,"payload":{"prompt":"Distinguish between bookkeeping and accounting, and state three similarities between them."},"answer":{"kind":"model_answer","itemId":"7259c50d-9dfc-55c9-8d3c-db3d473bd371","sourceKey":"guide-answer:1","payload":{"body":"Define bookkeeping (collection and accurate recording of financial transactions) and accounting (recording plus analysing, summarising, interpreting and communicating to users). Differences: scope (narrow vs wide), task (recording vs interpreting and reporting), stage (first vs follows on), judgement (routine vs professional), end product (ledgers and trial balance vs financial statements). Similarities: both deal with money transactions, both use double entry and the same concepts, both use the same source documents and records."}}},{"kind":"theory_question","itemId":"dd0ea6f1-f5ac-57be-aaf9-41d68ca7b529","sourceKey":"guide-theory:2","position":2,"payload":{"prompt":"Tunde's opening capital was ₦400,000. During the year he introduced a further ₦50,000, made a profit of ₦120,000 and took drawings of ₦35,000. His liabilities at the year end were ₦90,000. Calculate his closing capital and total assets, and give the double entry for the drawings if taken in cash."},"answer":{"kind":"model_answer","itemId":"56703ecf-6425-596b-8374-d4fde0d5c643","sourceKey":"guide-answer:2","payload":{"body":"Closing capital = 400,000 + 50,000 + 120,000 − 35,000 = ₦535,000. Total assets = capital + liabilities = 535,000 + 90,000 = ₦625,000. Drawings: Dr Drawings ₦35,000, Cr Cash ₦35,000."}}},{"kind":"theory_question","itemId":"4ce45e99-da86-5da6-b297-bdedc23f7d28","sourceKey":"guide-theory:3","position":3,"payload":{"prompt":"On 1 Ma$study_guide$);
insert into private.study_guide_chunks (guide, ordinal, chunk) values ('study_guide_acc101', 34, $study_guide$y 2026, Dupe Enterprises (a provisions trader) (a) bought goods for resale on credit from Musa Ltd, list price ₦500,000 less 4% trade discount, and (b) bought a delivery van on credit from Lagos Motors for ₦900,000. State the book of original entry for each and the double entry."},"answer":{"kind":"model_answer","itemId":"612afe73-daed-5346-bf33-6945383e5d1a","sourceKey":"guide-answer:3","payload":{"body":"• (a) Trade discount = 4% × ₦500,000 = ₦20,000; net = ₦480,000. Book: purchases day book (source: purchase invoice). Dr purchases ₦480,000 (as part of the day book total); Cr Musa Ltd ₦480,000 in the purchases ledger. The ₦20,000 is not recorded.\n• (b) Book: general journal, since it is a non-current asset bought on credit. Dr delivery van ₦900,000; Cr Lagos Motors ₦900,000. Narration: \"Being delivery van bought on credit from Lagos Motors.\""}}},{"kind":"theory_question","itemId":"53434749-726b-5e0b-be5e-34804c05c538","sourceKey":"guide-theory:4","position":4,"payload":{"prompt":"Bola, a sole trader, buys 40 bags of rice for ₦800,000 and sells 30 bags on credit for ₦900,000 in December; the customer pays in January. In December she also takes one bag home for her family. Calculate gross profit for December and name the concept that applies to (i) the credit sale, (ii) the cost charged, (iii) the bag taken home."},"answer":{"kind":"model_answer","itemId":"8738a68b-7f51-5a0d-a0e3-06c7c7845942","sourceKey":"guide-answer:4","payload":{"body":"Cost per bag = ₦800,000 ÷ 40 = ₦20,000. Cost of goods sold = 30 × ₦20,000 = ₦600,000. Gross profit = ₦900,000 − ₦600,000 = ₦300,000. (i) Realisation: revenue is recognised in December when the goods are sold, not in January when cash arrives. (ii) Matching: only the cost of the 30 bags sold is set against December's sales. (iii) Business entity: the bag (₦20,000 at cost) is drawings, reducing her capital, not a business expense. The 9 bags left (₦180,000) are carried forward as closing inventory."}}},{"kind":"theory_question","itemId":"36fbc19d-4693-51d1-80e3-2a350929ed49","sourceKey":"guide-theory:5","position":5,"payload":{"prompt":"On 31 March 2026 Tola's cash book shows an overdraft of ₦25,000. Bank charges of ₦2,000 are not in the cash book, and the bank received a dividend of ₦10,000 for Tola. Unpresented cheques total ₦30,000 and a lodgement of ₦12,000 is not yet credited. Prepare the adjusted cash book balance and a BRS starting from it."},"answer":{"kind":"model_answer","itemId":"e0a321bb-23b3-550d-8684-9c79c97ded3c","sourceKey":"guide-answer:5","payload":{"body":"• Adjusted cash book: overdraft b/d ₦25,000 (credit side) + bank charges ₦2,000 (credit) − dividend ₦10,000 (debit) = overdraft ₦17,000, balance c/d on the debit side.\n• BRS: balance as per adjusted cash book (₦17,000); add unpresented cheques ₦30,000 = ₦13,000; less uncredited lodgement ₦12,000 = ₦1,000 in favour, the balance as per bank statement."}}},{"kind":"theory_question","itemId":"9337c72e-7039-5a1b-bae4-f70ef92747f3","sourceKey":"guide-theory:6","position":6,"payload":{"prompt":"The debit total of a trial balance exceeds the credit total by ₦900. Two errors are found: (i) electricity of ₦2,100, correctly entered in the cash book, was posted to the electricity account as ₦1,200; (ii) discount received of ₦1,800 was entered in the supplier's account but omitted from the discount received account. Give the correcting journal entries and the suspense account."},"answer":{"kind":"model_answer","itemId":"be080fff-8144-5d40-823c-8d8b2c6d4661","sourceKey":"guide-answer:6","payload":{"body":"The credit side was short, so suspense opens with a credit of ₦900.\n(i) Dr Electricity ₦900, Cr Suspense ₦900 (electricity under-posted).\n(ii) Dr Suspense ₦1,800, Cr Discount received ₦1,800 (one side omitted).\nSuspense: Dr discount received ₦1,800 = Cr (difference ₦900 + electricity ₦900) ₦1,800. The account clears to nil."}}},{"kind":"theory_question","itemId":"3a084f8a-6765-5889-bce3-75ecfebfe436","sourceKey":"guide-theory:7","position":7,"payload":{"prompt":"On 1 January rates prepaid were ₦4,000. During the year ₦30,000 was paid. At 31 December rates of ₦6,000 were owing. Calculate the rates charge for the year and state how the ₦6,000 appears in the balance sheet."},"answer":{"kind":"model_answer","itemId":"22442e2b-aaec-591f-a8bf-02570957ce26","sourceKey":"guide-answer:7","payload":{"body":"Charge = 4,000 + 30,000 + 6,000 = ₦40,000 to profit and loss. The ₦6,000 owing is an accrued expense, shown as a current liability. Entry for the accrual: Dr Rates, Cr Accrued rates."}}},{"kind":"theory_question","itemId":"0cc6c0e3-721d-51f2-9873-a355d6fd11c4","sourceKey":"guide-theory:8","position":8,"payload":{"prompt":"Distinguish between gross profit and net profit. Then compute both from: sales ₦500,000, returns inwards ₦20,000, opening stock ₦60,000, purchases ₦300,000, carriage inwards ₦10,000, carriage outwards ₦8,000, closing stock ₦70,000, rent ₦40,000, drawings ₦25,000."},"answer":{"kind":"model_answer","itemId":"c48d9574-e6d8-5b4e-9397-e0473d3d7bba","sourceKey":"guide-answer:8","payload":{"body":"Gross profit is net sales less cost of sales (found in the trading account); net profit is gross profit plus other income less all expenses (found in the profit and loss account). Net sales = 500,000 − 20,000 = ₦480,000. Cost of sales = 60,000 + 300,000 + 10,000 − 70,000 = ₦300,000. Gross profit = ₦180,000. Net profit = 180,000 − 8,000 (carriage outwards) − 40,000 (rent) = ₦132,000. Drawings of ₦25,000 are deducted from capital, not from profit."}}}]}$study_guide$);
